#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "421b9396-2a7b-5c45-897a-9de5f52683de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0042ce26-8793-5690-b4ab-9f018afe979a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10459" :question/problem "The function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4}{(x-1)^2}$ is defined for $x >1$. Find $f^{-1}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{4}{(x-1)^2}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4}{(y-1)^2}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (4)/((y - 1)^{2}) \\\\\nx \\cdot (y - 1)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n(y - 1)^{2} &= \\frac{4}{x} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(y - 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{x}} \\\\\n\\mid y - 1 \\mid &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y > 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid y-1 \\mid = y-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} + 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7266d5c7-67b9-59db-b994-07dcb143af1f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Simple Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9b149c51-015f-5346-9fca-8da406d0a79a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10460" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-x}{x+3}$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{2-x}{x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{2-y}{y+3}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{2 - y}{y + 3} \\\\\nx(y + 3) &= 2 - y \\\\\nxy + 3x &= 2 - y \\\\\nxy + y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny(x + 1) &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{x+1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcedd9d0-f87c-58b2-9ef1-c9fa0ef2e5c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f6ab6606-77ce-583a-9712-175825864612" :question/math-academy-id "e-10461" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$, defined as\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{kx + 6}{x-4}, \\qquad x\\neq 4$\nhas an inverse function given by\n$f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{4x+6}{x-2}, \\qquad x\\neq 2$.\nWhat is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{kx + 6}{x-4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{ky+6}{y-4}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{ky + 6}{y - 4} \\\\\nx(y - 4) &= ky + 6 \\\\\nxy - 4x &= ky + 6 \\\\\nxy - ky &= 4x + 6 \\\\\ny(x - k) &= 4x + 6 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4x + 6}{x - k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{4x+6}{x-k}\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=2$."}]}
